course: dictionary
lesson: 9478
title: Formal words and the law
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Statutory is a formal word, but you can work out its meaning from the idea of a statute, which is a law written down by a parliament. So statutory means set by law, or required by the official rules. «The Years 5–6 statutory list in England has about 100 words» tells you this list is one that must be followed, not one a child chooses for fun.
explain: Always check your meaning in the sentence. Swap in "required by law" for statutory and see if it still makes sense: "The Years 5–6 required-by-law list in England has about 100 words" works, so your meaning is right.
explain: Other clues help too. «Statutory sick pay» is pay that the law says an employer must give to a worker who is ill. «Statutory assessments» are tests the law says must be done. In each case, the word tells you something is compulsory, not optional.
fact: A statute is a written law, made by a parliament.
fact: «Statutory» means set by law, or required by the official rules.
fact: «Statutory sick pay» is money the law says an employer must pay a worker who is ill.
fact: «Statutory assessments» are tests the law says must be done, such as some tests in England.
fact: Statutory is a formal word, so you will often meet it in letters, news and exam questions.
exercise: «The Years 5–6 statutory list in England has about 100 words.» What does statutory mean here? | Very old and dusty | Written in green ink | *Set by law, so it must be used | Chosen by a child for fun
exercise: Ella reads a letter: "You are entitled to statutory sick pay." What does statutory suggest? | Given only on Sundays | *Required by law | Hidden in a box | Given only if you ask nicely
exercise: Tom says statutory is just a fancy word for "optional". What is the best answer? | Wrong, it means very short | Right, it means the same as funny | Right, you can choose whether to do it | *Wrong, it means something the law or official rules require, not optional
test: Which word is closest in meaning to statutory, as used here? | playful | *required by law | quiet | sparkling
test: Which of these is a statutory assessment? | *A test the law says must be done | A story we like to read | A game we pick for fun | A holiday we choose
exam: Why would a formal letter about school tests use the word statutory? | Because it is the shortest word to spell | *Because it means required by law, so it is precise | Because it is a silly word that makes the letter funny | Because it means happy and friendly
